answer for every UI font type, and finish the font descriptor API

A sixth application, Money Manager EX, is wxWidgets over Carbon and AppKit rather than Cocoa, and it
found two gaps in one launch.

kCTStrokeColorAttributeName WAS NOT DEFINED. Its pair, kCTStrokeWidthAttributeName, was. A two level
namespace binary resolves DATA symbols at load, so one absent CFStringRef aborted the process in dyld
before any window existed.

AND CTFontCreateUIFontForLanguage ANSWERED FOR TWO TYPES OUT OF TWENTY. KTFont_FT handled
kCTFontMenuTitleFontType and kCTFontMenuItemFontType and returned nil for everything else, including
the plain system font.

AppKit hid that completely: +[NSFont _uiFontOfType:size:fallbackName:] catches the nil and falls back
to San Francisco by name, so no Cocoa application ever saw it. A caller that goes to CoreText
DIRECTLY gets the nil. wxWidgets put it straight into a CFDictionary and the process died in

  CoreFoundation HALT in __CFTypeCollectionRetain, called from __CFBasicHashAddValue
    3 wxFontRefData::SetFont(__CTFont const*)
    5 wxOSXSystemFontsCacheModule::Get(wxOSXSystemFont)
    7 wxWindow::DoSetWindowVariant(wxWindowVariant)
    8 wxNonOwnedWindow::Create(...)

eleven frames from anything about fonts.

THE MENU TYPES KEEP 12 AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. A Mac uses 14, and raising it here was MEASURED on the
roster: every menu bar title moved right, the panes shifted, and a fixed test coordinate that had
been landing on View landed on Authors. Correcting the menu size deserves its own before and after
captures, not a side effect of this. The size table only applies when the caller passes 0, so an
explicit size is untouched either way.

NOT DISTINGUISHED YET, and said in the code rather than left to be discovered: the Emphasized
variants come back in the same face as their plain siblings, so an emphasized system font is the
right SIZE and not bold. That beats nil, which is what a caller cannot handle at all.

ALSO, three font descriptor functions mmex needs and this port had never written. A descriptor here
IS its attribute dictionary, which is what makes them short: CopyAttributes is a dictionary copy,
CreateCopyWithAttributes is an overlay, and CreateCopyWithSymbolicTraits rewrites the symbolic trait
inside the kCTFontTraitsAttribute sub-dictionary, honouring the MASK so bits the caller did not
mention keep the value they had.

MEASURED, on the five application roster, every capture LOOKED at and compared against the run before
this change: iA Writer opens the View menu with its full item list, Swift Publisher selects Brochures,
MoneyMoney shows cider in its search field, iTerm2 shows the command at the prompt, LibreOffice has
the text on the page with 4 words 23 characters. No difference from the previous captures.

